000\n","0.05\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 1.2 F strings"],"metadata":{"id":"WF2OlvOmiMX4"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["F strings in Python allow us to easily format strings by embedding expressions inside curly braces {}. This makes it easier to insert variables and expressions into strings without having to use concatenation or string formatting methods like % or .format().\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"IKhzZpmgiMYC"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":6,"metadata":{"id":"0lpfWzTEiMYC","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727107664662,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":408,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"d8cdcf42-fb67-4392-9300-347eda7f88bd"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Stock Name: AAPL\n","Stock Price: $150.25\n","Number of Shares: 100\n"]}],"source":["# Finance example using F strings\n","stock_name = \"AAPL\"\n","stock_price = 150.25\n","num_shares = 100\n","\n","# Displaying the information using F strings\n","print(f\"Stock Name: {stock_name}\")\n","print(f\"Stock Price: ${stock_price}\")\n","print(f\"Number of Shares: {num_shares}\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"GJRkvxPXiMYD"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Create the following variables (company_name, revenue, and expenses) and display their values. Use some hypothetical values."],"metadata":{"id":"BLms6Kq_iMYD"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Financial Report using F strings (company_name, revenue, expenses)\n","company_name = \"XYZ Corp\"\n","revenue = 10000\n","expenses = 5000\n","\n","# Displaying the financial report using F strings\n","# Write your code here\n","print(f\"Company Name : {company_name}\")\n","print(f\"Revenue : ${revenue}\")\n","print(f\"Expenses : ${expenses}\")"],"metadata":{"id":"3yyYltZliMYD","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727107916935,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":459,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"82eddfe7-c962-40dc-a3df-2da1a5298ff9"},"execution_count":7,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Company Name : XYZ Corp\n","Revenue : $10000\n","Expenses : $5000\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 1.3 Basic Arithmetic Operations"],"metadata":{"id":"Psqa9vlt3EJd"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Performing basic arithmetic operations in Python allows us to manipulate numerical values. This includes addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. These operations are fundamental in mathematical calculations and data manipulation.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"oierTP-W4UAP"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":8,"metadata":{"id":"HCymqyY03EJe","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727108109417,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":473,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"9a145f42-bfed-479f-c42d-589b7d8bda21"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Future value of the investment after 5 years: $12762.815625000003\n"]}],"source":["# Define variables for financial calculations\n","initial_investment = 10000\n","annual_return_rate = 0.05\n","years = 5\n","\n","# Calculate the future value of the investment\n","future_value = initial_investment * (1 + annual_return_rate) ** years\n","\n","# Print the future value of the investment\n","print(f\"Future value of the investment after 5 years: ${future_value}\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"Zl7O5Gra5CGU"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Calculate the total cost of purchasing 5 items that cost $20 each, including tax at a rate of 8%.\n","\n","Hint: First, calculate the total cost of the items before tax by multiplying the cost of each item by the number of items. Then, calculate the total cost including tax by adding the tax amount (which is 8% of the total cost before tax) to the total cost before tax.\n"],"metadata":{"id":"1rJbA9KQ4l2I"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Define variables for financial calculations\n","item_cost = 20\n","num_items = 5\n","tax_rate = 0.08\n","\n","# Calculate the total cost including tax: (item_cost * num_items) * (1 + tax_rate)\n","# Write your code here\n","total_cost = (item_cost * num_items)*(1+tax_rate)\n","# Print the total cost\n","print(\"Total cost of purchasing 5 items with tax: $\", total_cost)"],"metadata":{"id":"UA0oA4BV4pEf","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727108203442,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":443,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"23dac24e-8cdd-4d19-a30d-00c1eed0acc7"},"execution_count":9,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total cost of purchasing 5 items with tax: $ 108.0\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 1.4 Data Type Conversion"],"metadata":{"id":"W2cd3-zahQwO"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Data type conversion in Python allows us to change the type of a variable from one data type to another. This is important because different data types are used for different purposes and operations in Python. We can convert data types when we need to perform operations that require variables of a specific type, or when we need to format data in a certain way. For example, we may need to convert a string to an integer in order to perform mathematical calculations, or convert a float to a string for display purposes."],"metadata":{"id":"UrLr0N5HhQwO"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":10,"metadata":{"id":"faGmkrmNhQwP","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727108341551,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":428,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"0f5bed46-a504-49ee-f126-542733aab7ce"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total expenses: 500\n"]}],"source":["# Convert a string to an integer for calculating total expenses\n","expense_str = \"500\"\n","expense_int = int(expense_str)\n","print(\"Total expenses:\", expense_int)"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Convert a float to a string for displaying stock price\n","stock_price_float = 123.45\n","stock_price_str = str(stock_price_float)\n","print(\"Stock price: $\" + stock_price_str)"],"metadata":{"id":"kFQSL-LG7n2A","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727108344846,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":429,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"0745d564-ec7f-4e70-e83b-0667b43bb114"},"execution_count":11,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Stock price: $123.45\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"k7cnKTCahQwP"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Perform data type conversion by converting a float to an integer for calculating annual interest rate. Given an annual interest rate of 3.75%, convert it to an integer for further calculations.\n","\n","Hint: You can multiply the float value by 100 to convert it to an integer representing percentage."],"metadata":{"id":"l75hqKK0hQwP"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Convert a float to an integer for calculating annual interest rate\n","annual_interest_rate_float = 3.75\n","annual_interest_rate_int = int(annual_interest_rate_float)\n","\n","print(\"Annual interest rate:\", annual_interest_rate_int, \"%\")"],"metadata":{"id":"-7sLRzonhQwQ","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727108506346,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":13,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"3ff722e9-18fb-4510-cad2-6d8ad251891a"},"execution_count":12,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Annual interest rate: 3 %\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## 2. Data Types and Variables in Python"],"metadata":{"id":"UpF7gEb3wZ_J"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 2.1 Numeric Data Types"],"metadata":{"id":"kVzdgNfiwZ_K"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Numeric data types in Python are used to represent numerical values such as integers and floating-point numbers. They allow us to perform mathematical operations and calculations in our code. These data types include int (integer) and float (floating-point number). It is important to understand numeric data types as they are fundamental for working with numerical data in Python.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"EpCpcDFzwZ_L"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"_rAnPR-_wZ_Y"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Define an integer variable\n","num1 = 10\n","\n","# Define a floating-point variable\n","num2 = 3.14\n","\n","# Perform addition operation\n","result_addition = num1 + num2\n","print(\"Addition result:\", result_addition)"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Perform multiplication operation\n","result_multiplication = num1 * num2\n","print(\"Multiplication result:\", result_multiplication)"],"metadata":{"id":"ixitHJQ37vs1"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"WtBmvUX1wZ_Z"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Perform a simple interest calculation using numeric data types in Python. Assume the principal amount is `$1000`, the interest rate is `5%`, and the time period is 2 years. Calculate the total amount after interest is applied.\n","To calculate simple interest: `total_amount = principal_amount * (1 + (interest_rate * time_period))`"],"metadata":{"id":"A-CsgOaFwZ_c"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Define the principal amount\n","principal = 1000\n","\n","# Define the interest rate\n","interest_rate = 0.05\n","\n","# Define the time period\n","time_period = 2\n","\n","# Calculate the total amount after interest: principal + (principal * interest_rate * time_period)\n","# Write your code here\n","total_amount = principal * (1 + (interest_rate * time_period))\n","print(\"Total amount after interest:\", total_amount)"],"metadata":{"id":"4Z3YNbLEwZ_m","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727108862027,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":471,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"b36f0fb8-54c3-475c-ce06-cbc745587cb6"},"execution_count":13,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total amount after interest: 1100.0\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 2.2 Lists and Tuples"],"metadata":{"id":"rVvpXEb3wZ_1"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Lists and Tuples in Python are data structures used to store multiple items in a single variable. Lists are mutable, meaning their elements can be changed, while Tuples are immutable, meaning their elements cannot be changed. Both Lists and Tuples can store different data types, including integers, strings, and even other Lists or Tuples."],"metadata":{"id":"r4YPPk_vwZ_2"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":14,"metadata":{"id":"oCSg1BQXwZ_2","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727109069831,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":435,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"56e12c87-35b4-45da-80b2-2d2593d9d5cd"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["110\n","GOOGL\n"]}],"source":["# Creating a list of stock prices\n","stock_prices = [100, 105, 110, 115, 120]\n","\n","# Creating a tuple of stock symbols\n","stock_symbols = ('AAPL', 'GOOGL', 'AMZN', 'MSFT', 'TSLA')\n","\n","# Printing the third stock price\n","print(stock_prices[2])\n","\n","# Printing the second stock symbol\n","print(stock_symbols[1])"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Explanation**: We create a list of stock prices using the values 100, 105, 110, 115, and 120. We then create a tuple of stock symbols with the values 'AAPL', 'GOOGL', 'AMZN', 'MSFT', and 'TSLA'. We print out the list of stock prices and the tuple of stock symbols."],"metadata":{"id":"O0fIx3urwZ_8"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"T6ibzDuBwZ_8"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Create a list of monthly expenses and a tuple of monthly incomes. We've calculated the total monthly expenses and the average monthly income."],"metadata":{"id":"LUeLoz4XwZ_9"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# List of monthly expenses\n","# Write your code here\n","expenses = [1000,5000,6700,1500,700]\n","print(expenses[2])\n","# Tuple of monthly incomes\n","# Write your code here\n","incomes = (500000,670000,689000,23456)\n","print(incomes[2])"],"metadata":{"id":"9nrLBNPEwZ_9","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727109350739,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":433,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"61393784-604f-498b-b490-b029304b80ec"},"execution_count":16,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["6700\n","689000\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Calculating total monthly expenses\n","total_expenses = sum(expenses)\n","\n","# Calculating average monthly income\n","average_income = sum(incomes) / len(incomes)\n","\n","print(\"Total monthly expenses:\", total_expenses)\n","print(\"Average monthly income:\", average_income)"],"metadata":{"id":"IWcdWeY18VcU","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727109600421,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":470,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"ffd7e83a-28b5-43bb-a843-0496a045193b"},"execution_count":17,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total monthly expenses: 14900\n","Average monthly income: 470614.0\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 2.3 Dictionaries and Sets"],"metadata":{"id":"WWVXQD_g3HWi"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Dictionaries and sets in Python are data structures that allow us to store collections of data. Dictionaries store items as key-value pairs where each key is associated with a value, while sets are unordered collections of unique elements and do not have key-value pairs.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"TK-Ry1Bt3HWj"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":18,"metadata":{"id":"Ic-9RiZU3HWj","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727109912248,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":464,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"96814fa9-ff6a-442b-df3b-096f4c27594b"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["The stock price of AAPL is: $150.25\n"]}],"source":["# Create a dictionary to store stock prices\n","stock_prices = {\n","    'AAPL': 150.25,\n","    'GOOGL': 2500.75,\n","    'AMZN': 3400.50,\n","    'MSFT': 300.60\n","}\n","\n","# Create a set to store the top performing stocks\n","top_performing_stocks = {'GOOGL', 'AMZN'}\n","\n","# Retrieve the stock price for 'AAPL' from the dictionary\n","aapl_price = stock_prices['AAPL']\n","print(f\"The stock price of AAPL is: ${aapl_price}\")"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Check if 'GOOGL' is in the set of top-performing stocks\n","if 'GOOGL' in top_performing_stocks:\n","    print(\"GOOGL is one of the top-performing stocks.\")"],"metadata":{"id":"yNno4Vgg9WAg","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727109926401,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":499,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"7bd1f269-c5c2-4388-dd9c-7599f826cbd4"},"execution_count":19,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["GOOGL is one of the top-performing stocks.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"ZdFKY8SL3HWk"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Create a dictionary to store the monthly returns of different stocks in percentages and print out the data.\n","\n","Consider using stock tickers as keys and monthly returns as values. Remember that dictionaries in Python use curly braces {} and key-value pairs separated by colons."],"metadata":{"id":"s4RkRYJ_3HWk"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Create a dictionary to store monthly returns of different stocks in percentages\n","monthly_returns = {\n","    'AAPL': 5.2,\n","    'GOOGL': 3.8,\n","    'AMZN': 6.5,\n","    'MSFT': 4.1\n","}\n","\n","# Print the value for the 'GOOGL' key\n","# Write your code here\n","if 'GOOGL' in monthly_returns :\n","  print(monthly_returns[\"GOOGL\"])"],"metadata":{"id":"b-nE16sb3HWk","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727110018333,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":458,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"837966b6-c927-42fa-895c-95075c367b1d"},"execution_count":20,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["3.8\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## 3. Control Structures and Loops in Python"],"metadata":{"id":"cENGk4CAwYo4"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 3.1 If-Else Statements"],"metadata":{"id":"X4ZFqulOwYo5"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["If-Else statements in Python allow us to make decisions in our code based on certain conditions. This control structure helps us to execute different blocks of code depending on whether a certain condition is true or false. If the condition is true, the code inside the 'if' block will be executed. If the condition is false, the code inside the 'else' block will be executed.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"kvpKz3uiwYo5"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":21,"metadata":{"id":"vWpeHioKwYo5","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727110363859,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":441,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"d1628fe0-1c9f-4e40-eb20-d3c6f6230ab6"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Hold or sell stocks.\n"]}],"source":["stock_price = 100\n","buy_price = 90\n","\n","if stock_price < buy_price:\n","    print(\"Buy more stocks!\")\n","else:\n","    print(\"Hold or sell stocks.\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"p5Xh-IEpwYo6"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Calculate the profit or loss percentage based on the purchase price and selling price of a stock. If the selling price is higher than the purchase price, calculate the profit percentage. If the selling price is lower than the purchase price, calculate the loss percentage.\n","\n","To calculate profit percentage: `((selling price - purchase price) / purchase price) * 100`. To calculate loss percentage: `((purchase price - selling price) / purchase price) * 100`."],"metadata":{"id":"jm9X0MlXwYo6"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["purchase_price = 90\n","selling_price = 100\n","\n","if selling_price > purchase_price :\n","    profit_percentage = ((selling_price - purchase_price) / purchase_price) * 100\n","    print(f\"Profit Percentage: {profit_percentage}%\")\n","else:\n","    loss_percentage = ((purchase_price - selling_price) / purchase_price) * 100\n","    print(f\"Loss Percentage: {loss_percentage}%\")"],"metadata":{"id":"0LSa37wQwYo6","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727111153335,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":468,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"67a14f0f-f1dd-4395-9acc-6d96a217eab6"},"execution_count":27,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Profit Percentage: 11.11111111111111%\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 3.2 El-if Statements"],"metadata":{"id":"i0JKR0-bwYo8"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["El-if statements in Python allow us to check multiple conditions in a structured way. This control structure is used when we have more than two possible outcomes to consider in our code. We use El-if statements when we want to check multiple conditions in a specific order, where each condition is only checked if the previous conditions were not met. This allows us to handle complex decision-making scenarios in our code.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"QXQ0SKYbwYo8"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"FhXq9ewUwYo9"},"outputs":[],"source":["stock_price = 100\n","\n","if stock_price < 50:\n","    print(\"Buy more stocks\")\n","elif stock_price >= 50 and stock_price < 100:\n","    print(\"Hold on to your stocks\")\n","else:\n","    print(\"Sell your stocks\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"Q1H5Wx6vwYo9"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Calculate the total profit based on the following conditions: If the revenue is greater than 1000 and expenses are less than 500, the profit is 500. If the revenue is less than or equal to 1000 and expenses are greater than or equal to 500, the profit is -200. Otherwise, the profit is 0.\n","\n","Use if, elif, and else statements to check the conditions in the specified order. Remember to consider the logic of the conditions to determine the correct profit outcome."],"metadata":{"id":"s79kuyDxwYo-"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["revenue = 1200\n","expenses = 400\n","\n","# Create the if statement\n","if revenue > 1000 and expenses < 500:\n","    profit = 500\n","# Write your code here\n","elif revenue >1000 and expenses >= 500 :\n","    profit = -200\n","else:\n","    profit = 0\n","\n","print(\"Total Profit:\", profit)"],"metadata":{"id":"Olsb2ZWOwYo-","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727111278131,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":449,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"b1665da7-5cfb-47bc-e744-de469e751845"},"execution_count":28,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total Profit: 500\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 3.3 For Loops"],"metadata":{"id":"LdecM0Mh1MlI"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["For loops in Python allow us to iterate over a sequence of elements, such as a list or a range of numbers. This is useful when we want to perform a certain action on each element in the sequence.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"CBuD-nSr1Mlb"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":29,"metadata":{"id":"iVLR9U8m1Mlb","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727111575175,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":430,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"2c164ef7-4c19-4e69-c9f0-39642945f533"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total portfolio value: 5950\n"]}],"source":["# Calculate the total value of a portfolio by summing the values of individual assets\n","portfolio = [1000, 2500, 500, 750, 1200]\n","total_value = 0\n","\n","for asset_value in portfolio:\n","    total_value += asset_value # total_value = total_value + asset_value\n","\n","print(\"Total portfolio value:\", total_value)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Explanation**: We initialize a list called \"portfolio\" with values representing the individual assets. We also initialize a variable \"total_value\" to store the sum of the asset values. We then use a for loop to iterate over each asset value in the portfolio list, adding it to the total value. Finally, we print out the total portfolio value by summing the individual asset values."],"metadata":{"id":"yI4ovT8O1Mlc"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"PARiprXG1Mlc"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Calculate the total interest earned from a savings account after 5 years with an annual interest rate of 3%.\n","\n","Create a variable to store the initial savings amount. Use a for loop to iterate over 5 years, calculating the interest earned each year by multiplying the savings amount by 0.03. Add the interest earned to the savings amount each year. Finally, print out the total interest earned after 5 years.\n","\n","Hints:\n","\n","- interest_earned = initial_balance * annual_interest_rate\n","- total_interest = total_interest + interest_earned\n","- initial_balance = initial_balance + interest_earned"],"metadata":{"id":"VSY80A891Mld"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Calculate the total interest earned from a savings account after 5 years with an annual interest rate of 3%\n","initial_balance = 1000\n","annual_interest_rate = 0.03\n","total_interest = 0\n","years = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n","\n","# Write your code here\n","for years in years :\n","  interest_earned = initial_balance * annual_interest_rate\n","  total_interest = total_interest + interest_earned\n","  initial_balance = initial_balance + interest_earned\n","  years += 1\n","print(\"Total interest earned after 5 years:\", total_interest)"],"metadata":{"id":"ZWcJVoly1Mld","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727111745788,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":14,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"d91002f8-90a8-4d0c-dd6d-a8d4695aec07"},"execution_count":30,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total interest earned after 5 years: 159.2740743\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 3.4 The Break and Continue Statements"],"metadata":{"id":"AoAdEya1nH6Z"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["The break and continue statements are control flow statements in Python that allow us to alter the flow of a loop. The break statement is used to exit a loop prematurely, while the continue statement is used to skip the rest of the current iteration and move on to the next one.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"74GMQ2VxnH6q"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"QbtjkrJsnH6q"},"outputs":[],"source":["# List of stock prices\n","stock_prices = [100, 110, 105, 120, 115, 130, 125]\n","\n","# Calculate the total value of stocks that are greater than 115\n","total_value = 0\n","for price in stock_prices:\n","    if price <= 115:\n","        continue  # Skip this iteration if price is less than or equal to 115\n","    total_value += price\n","    if total_value > 300:\n","        break  # Exit the loop if total value exceeds 300\n","\n","print(\"Total value of stocks greater than 115:\", total_value)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Explanation**: We initialize a list called stock_prices with values representing stock prices. We then set total_value to 0. We iterate through each price in stock_prices. If the price is less than or equal to 115, we skip to the next iteration. If the price is greater than 115, we add it to the total_value. If the total_value exceeds 300, we exit the loop. Finally, we print the total value of stocks greater than 115."],"metadata":{"id":"DvJSO1J-nH6r"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"-kW312g2nH6r"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Calculate the total profit generated from a list of stock prices. If the profit exceeds 500, stop the calculation and print the total profit.\n","\n","Use a for loop to iterate through the list of stock prices. Use a variable to keep track of the total profit. Use the continue statement to skip calculations for prices less than or equal to 100. Use the break statement to exit the loop if the total profit exceeds 500.\n"],"metadata":{"id":"X8EZmSU5nH6r"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# List of stock prices\n","stock_prices = [100, 110, 105, 120, 115, 130, 125]\n","\n","# Calculate the total profit generated from stock prices\n","total_profit = 0\n","for price in stock_prices:\n","    profit = price - 100  # Assuming the initial stock price is 100\n","    if profit <= 0:\n","      continue\n","    total_profit += profit\n","    if total_profit > 500:\n","      break\n","\n","print(\"Total profit generated from stock prices:\", total_profit)"],"metadata":{"id":"3yIZipClnH6s","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727113541378,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":469,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"cbcf0469-ca73-4cb7-fb75-2274bc4ef023"},"execution_count":31,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total profit generated from stock prices: 105\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## 4. Functions and Modules"],"metadata":{"id":"cF1gahsawbcO"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 4.1 Creating a Basic Function"],"metadata":{"id":"ZmUD1qP8wbce"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Creating a basic function in Python allows us to define a block of code that performs a specific task. This function can then be called multiple times throughout our program, reducing code duplication and improving readability. Functions are essential for breaking down complex problems into smaller, more manageable tasks."],"metadata":{"id":"sqYD5M_swbce"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":32,"metadata":{"id":"bpQ1kgsfwbcf","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727113551225,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":430,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}}},"outputs":[],"source":["# Define a function to calculate the compound interest\n","def calculate_compound_interest(principal, rate, time):\n","    interest = principal * (1 + rate)**time - principal\n","    return interest"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"9Lnahl5hwbcf"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Create a function in Python to calculate the total return on investment (ROI) given the initial investment amount, final investment amount, and the number of years the investment was held.\n","\n","Hint: Use the formula `(final_investment_amount - initial_investment_amount) / initial_investment_amount * 100` to calculate the ROI. Define a function called `calculate_roi(initial_investment_amount, final_investment_amount, years)` that takes in these three parameters and returns the calculated `ROI`."],"metadata":{"id":"82zZtGDYwbcg"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Define a function to calculate the total return on investment (ROI) - calculate_roi()\n","# Write your code here\n","def calculate_roi(initial_investment_amount, final_investment_amount, years) :\n","  Roi = (final_investment_amount - initial_investment_amount) / initial_investment_amount * 100\n","  return Roi\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"526atUqEwbcg","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727113654624,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":504,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}}},"execution_count":33,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 4.2 Return Statements"],"metadata":{"id":"AefZVOkIwbcj"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Return statements in Python allow us to return a value from a function back to the caller. This is important because it allows us to perform a calculation or operation within a function and then pass the result back to the main program for further processing."],"metadata":{"id":"CH21kfKkwbck"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":34,"metadata":{"id":"-OH2xAqOwbck","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727113669904,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":443,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"a392f3ad-3169-484c-ec27-c4895516ad74"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Stock return: 20.00%\n"]}],"source":["def calculate_stock_return(initial_price, final_price):\n","    return (final_price - initial_price) / initial_price\n","\n","# Example usage\n","initial_price = 100\n","final_price = 120\n","stock_return = calculate_stock_return(initial_price, final_price)\n","print(\"Stock return: {:.2%}\".format(stock_return))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"KrUgWn0zwbck"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Calculate the annualized return of an investment given the initial investment amount, final investment amount, and number of years the investment was held. Remember to use the formula for annualized return: `((final_investment / initial_investment) ** (1/years)) - 1`.\n","\n","To calculate the annualized return, use the formula provided and make sure to return the result back to the caller using the 'return' statement."],"metadata":{"id":"gkU71W_Iwbcl"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["def calculate_annualized_return(initial_investment, final_investment, years):\n","    return ((final_investment / initial_investment) ** (1/years)) - 1\n","\n","# Example usage\n","# Write your code here\n","calculate_annualized_return(500,1000,2)\n"],"metadata":{"id":"BttufwRiwbcl","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727113749033,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":444,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"64bb2855-3e55-4b90-e172-47daf94c4c78"},"execution_count":35,"outputs":[{"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":["0.41421356237309515"]},"metadata":{},"execution_count":35}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### 4.3 Importing Modules"],"metadata":{"id":"i32f4Uzkpl6v"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Importing modules in Python allows us to access functions, classes, and variables defined in another file. This allows us to reuse code, organize our code into separate files, and avoid writing the same code multiple times."],"metadata":{"id":"q1J2X9GSpl68"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":36,"metadata":{"id":"79u1CMMGpl68","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727113760387,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":419,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"e80b5807-a405-4ddc-84d9-7f22875d3165"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Square root of 16 is 4.0\n"]}],"source":["# Importing the math module to use mathematical functions\n","import math\n","\n","# Using the sqrt function from the math module to calculate the square root of a number\n","num = 16\n","result = math.sqrt(num)\n","print(\"Square root of\", num, \"is\", result)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Explanation**: We import the math module to access mathematical functions. We then use the sqrt function from the math module to calculate the square root of the number 16. The result is stored in a variable called result."],"metadata":{"id":"zn1vt0OGpl69"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["#### <font color=\"green\">Challenge</font>"],"metadata":{"id":"zFvmC8Zjpl69"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["You are working on a financial project and need to calculate the compound interest for an investment. Create a Python script that imports the 'math' module and uses the 'pow' function to calculate the compound interest for an initial investment of $1000, an annual interest rate of 5%, and a period of 3 years.\n","\n","Hint: The math.pow() takes in two arguments: 1 + annual_interest_rate and period."],"metadata":{"id":"Nl_uxqLDpl69"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Importing the math module to use mathematical functions\n","# Write your code here\n","\n","# Calculating the compound interest for an initial investment of $1000, an annual interest rate of 5%, and a period of 3 years\n","initial_investment = 1000\n","annual_interest_rate = 0.05\n","period = 3\n","\n","# Using the pow function from the math module to calculate compound interest\n","compound_interest = initial_investment * math.pow(1 +  annual_interest_rate , period)\n","\n","# Displaying the calculated compound interest\n","# Write your code here\n","print(f'The compounded interest is {compound_interest}')"],"metadata":{"id":"cPMQdOyppl69","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1727113866715,"user_tz":-180,"elapsed":435,"user":{"displayName":"Kelvin Mugwe","userId":"01853534553004304250"}},"outputId":"d9b31bd7-df98-4b9a-e3db-d77b977e15ca"},"execution_count":37,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["The compounded interest is 1157.6250000000002\n"]}]}]}